{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I have to give a presentation tomorrow and I am completely freezing up. What if I forget everything?", "response": "That nervous energy is so normal — it actually means you care, and that alone makes you a good speaker. Try this: practice just once with someone you trust, then step away from it. Your brain will keep processing in the background. Tomorrow, focus on helping your audience understand something useful rather than performing perfectly. And remember, even if you stumble, people are far more forgiving than you think. You have got this."}
{"prompt": "I feel so lonely lately. Everyone seems to have their group of friends and I am just watching from the outside.", "response": "I hear you, and I want you to know that this ache you are feeling is deeply human — not a personal failure. Connection takes time and a little courage, but it grows in small steps. Have you thought about joining one small community around something you already enjoy? A board game group, a book club, even a weekly cooking class? Shared interests make starting conversations feel natural. And be patient with yourself — the right people will notice your warmth when you show up as you are."}
{"prompt": "My boss dumped a huge project on me with an impossible deadline. I am drowning and dont know how to ask for help without looking incompetent.", "response": "First, take a quiet breath — you are not drowning, you are overwhelmed, and there is a big difference. Overwhelm is fixable. Could you try a short meeting with your boss framed as a planning conversation? Say something like, I want to make sure I deliver this well. To hit the deadline, I need to prioritize these three areas. What should I deprioritize to make that work? That is not looking incompetent — that is professional maturity. And please, be kind to yourself tonight. You are doing hard work."}
{"prompt": "I snapped at my partner earlier and now I feel terrible. I dont know how to fix it.", "response": "The fact that you feel terrible shows what matters most — that you value this relationship. A sincere repair goes a long way. Try saying something like, I am really sorry for how I spoke earlier. I was overwhelmed and I took it out on you, which was not fair. Can we try again? You do not need to have a perfect script, just speak from the heart. They will likely feel the sincerity, and this moment can actually bring you closer. Relationships are built on these repairs."}
{"prompt": "I just broke up with someone I was with for three years and my world feels completely upside down right now.", "response": "Three years is a huge chapter to close, and it is completely okay that everything feels sideways right now. Grief does not care how logical the breakup was — it just wants space to be felt. Please give yourself permission to not be okay today. Lean on one or two trusted friends, keep eating even when you do not feel like it, and avoid making big life decisions for a few weeks. This pain is temporary even when it feels endless. You will find your footing again, one gentle day at a time."}
